WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.784 --> 00:00:04.214
GOOGLE CLOUD NEXT '20: ON AIR

00:00:04.214 --> 00:00:05.399
WHAT'S NEXT

00:00:05.399 --> 00:00:07.699
HIDDEN FORCES:
WHAT'S NEXT IN ENTERPRISE IT

00:00:08.379 --> 00:00:13.146
Hello, and thanks for tuning in
to the final keynote of Next OnAir 2020.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.551
Today I'm going to talk to you
about the upcoming revolution in cloud

00:00:17.551 --> 00:00:21.122
one of the rare cases
where picking the right path today

00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:24.624
helps you both in the short term
and over the next two decades.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.029
But first a bit of background,
so bear with me for a few minutes.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.512
I'm Urs Hölzle.

00:00:30.512 --> 00:00:34.696
And in my 21 years of leading
the technical infrastructure teams at Google

00:00:34.696 --> 00:00:36.115
I have had the good fortune

00:00:36.115 --> 00:00:38.738
to work with many
of the world's smartest engineers

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:40.975
software developers, and business leaders.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:44.048
Not just at Google
but at customers, our partners

00:00:44.048 --> 00:00:46.670
and in academia
and the open-source community.

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:49.658
Working together, we've been able to take on

00:00:49.658 --> 00:00:55.695
one of the biggest problems in IT,
the sorry state of enterprise innovation.

00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:02.089
The sad fact is today
much of enterprise IT is like a dinosaur

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:05.517
big but slow and in danger of dying out.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:08.903
And we see all the innovation
that consumers enjoy

00:01:08.903 --> 00:01:11.420
and we wonder, why can't we have that too?

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:14.608
So it's easy to get depressed
about the future of IT

00:01:14.608 --> 00:01:17.258
and worry about its extinction.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:20.539
But it wasn't always like this.

00:01:20.606 --> 00:01:23.851
When I was a teenager,
back in the last millennium

00:01:23.851 --> 00:01:28.598
the best technology for
pretty much anything was in the enterprise.

00:01:29.054 --> 00:01:32.593
Big companies
had the best computers, the best databases

00:01:32.593 --> 00:01:34.098
even the best cameras.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:41.204
But starting about 25 years ago, around 1995
something extraordinary happened.

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.104
For the first time ever

00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:47.508
consumers began to enjoy innovation
at an unprecedented speed

00:01:47.508 --> 00:01:49.976
literally unheard of in human history.

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:53.857
Enterprise technology,
always the leader in the past

00:01:53.857 --> 00:01:55.501
began to fall behind.

00:01:55.949 --> 00:02:01.062
Consumers got webmail, search,
internet maps, cloud-based storage

00:02:01.062 --> 00:02:06.660
and then machine learning, real-time video,
awesome user experiences, automated backups

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.159
products that just kept getting better.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:15.233
Enterprise technology, meantime,
continued at its old, slower pace.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.017
What caused this reversal
in technology leadership?

00:02:19.306 --> 00:02:23.215
Do consumers spend more on technology now
and enterprises spend less?

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.378
No.

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:27.757
Did consumer software engineers
become much more talented?

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.132
No.

00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:33.015
Is there a magic quantum hyperspace
innovation accelerator

00:02:33.015 --> 00:02:34.989
that only works for consumer products?

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:36.186
Of course not.

00:02:36.957 --> 00:02:42.296
What did happen was a new way
of seeing and building technology

00:02:42.296 --> 00:02:45.558
and for a long time,
the enterprise has missed that way.

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.935
With the invention of the web

00:02:47.935 --> 00:02:50.838
there was a programming surface,
HTML

00:02:50.838 --> 00:02:54.275
that allowed
any application developer or SaaS vendor

00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:59.900
to deliver software to any customer,
and all these customers look the same.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.516
They all run on the same platform.

00:03:02.516 --> 00:03:05.419
And then 10 years ago
with Android and iPhone

00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:07.405
the same thing happened to phones.

00:03:07.988 --> 00:03:11.325
In both cases, the stacks auto-update

00:03:11.325 --> 00:03:16.243
so everyone is on the same version
of the same stack all the time.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.600
No different versions,
just a single powerful experience

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.911
usually on open standards
for maximum velocity.

00:03:23.737 --> 00:03:25.239
And with this approach

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:27.608
developers spend more time
on programming

00:03:27.608 --> 00:03:31.612
without having to deal with a myriad
of different versions at customers.

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:35.387
They can use practices
like continuous deployment and integration

00:03:35.387 --> 00:03:38.452
which rolls out small releases
more frequently

00:03:38.452 --> 00:03:41.896
starting with small sets of users
to make sure they're safe.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:46.970
They find bugs with
the first percent of users and fix them fast.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.850
And then the new release
can go out to all users.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:53.976
And that's low risk because they're all
on the same version of the stack.

00:03:54.768 --> 00:03:58.038
Let's pause here for a second
because this is the crucial point.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:03.978
If we find bugs early in the rollout
with 1% of users

00:04:03.978 --> 00:04:05.743
then to the rest of the users

00:04:05.743 --> 00:04:08.751
it looks like the bug rate
is 100 times smaller.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:13.687
Services just work with a hundred times
higher perceived quality

00:04:13.687 --> 00:04:15.670
so there's no need to fear upgrades

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:19.426
and it's no wonder that consumers
don't demand to qualify new releases

00:04:19.426 --> 00:04:20.627
before they're installed.

00:04:20.814 --> 00:04:24.407
They assume they work
because they almost always do.

00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:29.453
So the obvious question is
why hasn't this happened for the enterprise?

00:04:29.453 --> 00:04:32.373
Why are even companies that move to the cloud

00:04:32.373 --> 00:04:34.978
often dissatisfied
with their rate of innovation

00:04:34.978 --> 00:04:38.484
the quality of
their operational visibility or their security?

00:04:39.406 --> 00:04:42.855
It's because enterprises are in a tough spot.

00:04:43.678 --> 00:04:46.320
We all use similar hardware and software

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.394
but we integrated them differently
and we all have our own version matrix.

00:04:50.924 --> 00:04:53.274
So it's mostly similar stuff on a macro level

00:04:53.274 --> 00:04:56.816
but each arrangement
is as unique as a snowflake.

00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:01.488
So each enterprise is a snowflake
with its own unique version mix.

00:05:02.569 --> 00:05:06.540
So when I'm a SaaS vendor
trying to update a new customer

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.843
it's literally the first time
I'm deploying that update

00:05:09.843 --> 00:05:11.748
into exactly this environment.

00:05:12.264 --> 00:05:14.124
So everyone is nervous about the update

00:05:14.124 --> 00:05:16.887
because things tend to break
on the first attempt.

00:05:17.484 --> 00:05:21.155
And even after I've updated
10,000 other companies

00:05:21.155 --> 00:05:25.555
the next one is like the first time
because it's a new snowflake.

00:05:26.226 --> 00:05:29.136
So my software quality
doesn't look great to the customer.

00:05:29.352 --> 00:05:32.358
Updates are expensive,
and they're slow and infrequent.

00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:37.912
So it's the snowflake that's killing
the enterprise dinosaur.

00:05:38.638 --> 00:05:43.744
And if we don't get rid of snowflakes
enterprise innovation is going to be doomed

00:05:43.744 --> 00:05:46.892
because we cannot evolve,
just like the dinosaurs.

00:05:47.514 --> 00:05:50.084
These snowflakes have made
an enormous difference

00:05:50.084 --> 00:05:52.252
to the slope of the innovation curve.

00:05:52.252 --> 00:05:56.023
When you assume just a 20%
additional friction from snowflakes

00:05:56.023 --> 00:06:01.009
compounded over 25 years
that's a factor of 100.

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:04.363
That's why consumer innovation
has surged ahead.

00:06:06.065 --> 00:06:07.468
So what is the answer?

00:06:07.468 --> 00:06:09.236
How can we allow enterprises

00:06:09.236 --> 00:06:12.406
to each create
their own unique value proposition

00:06:12.406 --> 00:06:15.442
but with automation,
scale, and standardization

00:06:15.442 --> 00:06:16.929
at consumer-like speed?

00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:22.619
Well, the enterprise needs
a software platform that is always up to date

00:06:22.619 --> 00:06:28.251
where everyone uses exactly the same stack,
so updates see exactly the same environment.

00:06:28.655 --> 00:06:30.270
It needs to be open-source

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.784
so improvements can happen
in any environment, with no vendor login.

00:06:34.329 --> 00:06:37.164
And the platform needs
to accommodate legacy systems too

00:06:37.164 --> 00:06:39.681
because nobody can afford
to rewrite everything.

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:45.194
And it needs to run on-prem
and in any cloud, wherever you want it.

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:48.311
This platform exists today.

00:06:48.942 --> 00:06:53.005
It's seeing impressive enterprise adoption
and its name is Anthos.

00:06:53.813 --> 00:06:56.417
We introduced Anthos a year ago at Next.

00:06:56.417 --> 00:06:58.617
And since then we've been working closely

00:06:58.617 --> 00:07:01.255
with a number of
developers, partners, and customers

00:07:01.255 --> 00:07:03.393
to make it even stronger and more capable.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:10.333
Recently, an independent study showed 
an almost 5-times ROI when deploying Anthos.

00:07:10.661 --> 00:07:12.228
That's not just an improvement.

00:07:12.228 --> 00:07:13.723
It's a small revolution.

00:07:14.471 --> 00:07:17.704
Anthos integrates
dozens of open-source packages

00:07:17.704 --> 00:07:20.007
including Kubernetes containers

00:07:20.007 --> 00:07:25.635
and provides them as a fully managed stack
not just on GCP, but across many clouds

00:07:25.635 --> 00:07:28.367
in your on-premise data center
and at the edge.

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:33.320
It's based on open-source APIs,
making it for clouds

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.881
what the Linux operating system
was for servers 20 years ago

00:07:36.881 --> 00:07:40.260
a safe choice to avoid locking,
with a rich ecosystem

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.185
and plenty of developers who know it.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:45.632
Using Anthos, you can keep
your server hardware

00:07:45.632 --> 00:07:47.301
your cloud provider of choice

00:07:47.301 --> 00:07:49.436
and run pretty much anything on top of it.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:54.096
That's as true for enterprises
with on-prem services and mainframes

00:07:54.096 --> 00:07:57.224
as it is for companies
that were born in the cloud.

00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:02.583
What all this gets you is the ability
to develop software

00:08:02.583 --> 00:08:04.418
faster than ever before

00:08:04.418 --> 00:08:07.821
to bring to your enterprise
the kind of speed of innovation

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:11.391
normally associated
only with consumer-oriented companies.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:17.635
But Anthos goes beyond
just making it easier to write new software.

00:08:17.905 --> 00:08:21.242
It helps you operate
and secure existing applications.

00:08:21.478 --> 00:08:25.305
The fact is most enterprises
don't have the time or energy

00:08:25.305 --> 00:08:27.363
to rewrite their existing applications.

00:08:27.907 --> 00:08:30.711
So you need a platform that meets you
where you are today

00:08:30.711 --> 00:08:34.414
with a complex mix of third-party
and home-grown applications

00:08:34.414 --> 00:08:36.049
commercial and open-source

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:39.105
and helps you bring
those systems into the future.

00:08:39.552 --> 00:08:41.955
And Anthos can help there as well.

00:08:41.955 --> 00:08:46.493
In fact, I'd argue
Anthos is even more valuable

00:08:46.493 --> 00:08:48.158
for existing applications.

00:08:48.917 --> 00:08:51.348
Take just one example, Java apps.

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:53.934
Organizations often have hundreds

00:08:53.934 --> 00:08:57.354
or even thousands
of Java enterprise applications.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.407
These applications
are important to the business

00:09:00.407 --> 00:09:03.387
but get relatively little attention
because they're mature.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.314
So they languish until they become
so difficult to maintain

00:09:07.314 --> 00:09:09.681
that they have to be rewritten from scratch.

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:15.355
Now, in the fastest case,
with Anthos' migration tools

00:09:15.355 --> 00:09:19.159
you can take those apps
and migrate them automatically

00:09:19.159 --> 00:09:20.624
moving them to containers.

00:09:21.201 --> 00:09:24.231
You can keep these containers on-prem
or move to the cloud.

00:09:24.231 --> 00:09:25.552
That's your choice.

00:09:26.099 --> 00:09:28.468
But you see an immediate return

00:09:28.468 --> 00:09:31.872
because you're reducing infrastructure
and licensing costs

00:09:31.872 --> 00:09:35.676
and you have lower operating
and maintenance costs too

00:09:35.676 --> 00:09:42.821
because Anthos make it simple to run,
monitor, secure and update applications.

00:09:44.296 --> 00:09:48.922
You can even apply these
same migration techniques to Windows apps

00:09:48.922 --> 00:09:52.282
enabling you to bring
legacy Windows Server apps to containers.

00:09:52.789 --> 00:09:57.711
This includes Windows Server 2008,
which recently reached end of support.

00:09:58.898 --> 00:10:02.603
A study conducted by Forrester found
that using Anthos

00:10:02.603 --> 00:10:06.450
application migrations
were 58% to 75% faster.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.145
Platform operating efficiency
improved up to 55%.

00:10:11.744 --> 00:10:16.357
And productivity for security tasks
improved up to 75%.

00:10:17.896 --> 00:10:22.122
But Anthos is not a dead end where
you park old applications.

00:10:22.122 --> 00:10:24.491
For the apps you continue to develop

00:10:24.491 --> 00:10:28.762
Anthos makes it easy to adopt
modern cloud-native development practices

00:10:28.762 --> 00:10:31.365
including automated testing and deployment

00:10:31.365 --> 00:10:33.967
reducing the time it takes
your development teams

00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:35.840
to safely roll out new versions.

00:10:36.569 --> 00:10:39.706
You even get the ability
to secure your software supply chain

00:10:39.706 --> 00:10:41.742
with automated security analysis

00:10:41.742 --> 00:10:45.589
such as vulnerability scanning
and secure assigned binaries.

00:10:46.112 --> 00:10:49.383
This kind of speed
with stability and security

00:10:49.383 --> 00:10:53.099
is what enables consumer-oriented companies
to be so innovative.

00:10:53.409 --> 00:10:56.116
And with Anthos, you can have that too.

00:10:57.423 --> 00:10:59.126
And every step of the way

00:10:59.126 --> 00:11:04.731
your operations team gets new insight
into systems performance and reliability

00:11:04.731 --> 00:11:09.269
including the ability to set and monitor
service-level objectives

00:11:09.269 --> 00:11:13.616
as well as getting a comprehensive view
of your application dependencies.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:19.212
All of this comes from using a system
that encapsulates the best practices

00:11:19.212 --> 00:11:22.082
learned from our own decades of experience

00:11:22.082 --> 00:11:26.219
in running containers at scale,
going all the way back to 2006

00:11:26.219 --> 00:11:29.067
when we first brought containers
into the Linux kernel.

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:33.694
And we've distilled all this learning
and experience into Anthos

00:11:33.694 --> 00:11:37.941
so your teams can follow practices
that have stood the test of time.

00:11:39.098 --> 00:11:42.035
And this is not just a future possibility.

00:11:42.035 --> 00:11:43.518
It is available today.

00:11:44.094 --> 00:11:47.199
In a previous keynote,
you heard about how organizations

00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:52.248
as diverse as the bank HSBC,
the retailer HEB, Telegraph Media Group

00:11:52.248 --> 00:11:55.357
and Major League Baseball
are all using Anthos.

00:11:56.415 --> 00:11:58.919
And of course, we're using it ourselves

00:11:58.919 --> 00:12:02.689
to bring our marquee analytic service
to data stored in AWS

00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:05.933
with BigQuery Omni
that was announced a few weeks ago.

00:12:05.933 --> 00:12:09.129
Anthos is seeing broad uptake
in the enterprise.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.967
That's testimony to both the proven
open-source products inside Anthos

00:12:13.967 --> 00:12:15.669
and the work done at Google

00:12:15.669 --> 00:12:18.324
and the great results
that enterprises are seeing.

00:12:19.872 --> 00:12:22.490
All of this changes
how you should see cloud.

00:12:22.909 --> 00:12:26.819
And it's not really about
the big three clouds, as most people think.

00:12:27.647 --> 00:12:30.615
Now, let me go back to history
to explain what I mean.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:36.362
In the 1990s, enterprise faced the new world
of PCs, workstations, and servers.

00:12:36.989 --> 00:12:42.467
And they agonized over choosing HP,
Sun, Compaq, or someone else.

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:46.333
It was a big deal,
because once they chose one

00:12:46.333 --> 00:12:49.936
they got the vendor's OS,
compilers, databases.

00:12:49.936 --> 00:12:51.538
Everything was different.

00:12:51.538 --> 00:12:54.191
That was the world in 1995.

00:12:55.074 --> 00:12:58.756
But less than a decade later,
everyone realized

00:12:58.756 --> 00:13:01.307
there were just two choices that mattered.

00:13:01.814 --> 00:13:06.752
Not server companies, but operating systems:
Linux and Windows Server.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:11.119
That's the choice that stayed
with you for two decades.

00:13:11.891 --> 00:13:15.950
And of these two, open-sourced Linux
became a powerful standard.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:19.466
And it has become more central
to enterprise computing

00:13:19.466 --> 00:13:21.545
every single year since then.

00:13:22.235 --> 00:13:25.468
Anthos offers the same
open-source answer for cloud

00:13:25.468 --> 00:13:28.675
across on-premise data centers,
hybrid environments

00:13:28.675 --> 00:13:31.733
single or multi-cloud,
and even telco edge environments.

00:13:32.178 --> 00:13:34.981
Choosing Anthos is like choosing Linux.

00:13:34.981 --> 00:13:39.653
Multi-platform, more economical,
more innovative, open

00:13:39.653 --> 00:13:41.738
lower risk, at lower cost.

00:13:43.623 --> 00:13:46.092
Choosing Anthos helps
enterprise developers

00:13:46.092 --> 00:13:48.695
write amazing new modern applications

00:13:48.695 --> 00:13:50.896
but it also gives brownfield applications

00:13:50.896 --> 00:13:54.054
an incremental path to connect
to a rich future.

00:13:54.786 --> 00:13:57.456
Some applications
can even be migrated automatically.

00:13:58.004 --> 00:14:01.586
But most importantly,
Anthos can be adopted incrementally

00:14:01.586 --> 00:14:04.215
one step at a time with low risk.

00:14:06.112 --> 00:14:09.249
Now, some in the industry have argued

00:14:09.249 --> 00:14:13.520
that this kind of portability
degrades the cloud experience

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:16.590
or leads to a least common
denominator approach.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:20.079
They want you to stay inside
one cloud all the time.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.292
But in fact, it's the opposite

00:14:24.344 --> 00:14:26.765
because Anthos standardizes things

00:14:26.765 --> 00:14:29.900
that do not differentiate
your business operations.

00:14:30.503 --> 00:14:33.773
When developers do not have to learn
everything from scratch

00:14:33.773 --> 00:14:39.205
when there is one way to develop, operate
and secure your workloads on any cloud

00:14:39.612 --> 00:14:42.398
you can focus on value-adding activities.

00:14:42.398 --> 00:14:46.837
You're free to choose the best-in-class tools
and services that are right for you

00:14:46.837 --> 00:14:50.765
and if those needs change,
you don't have to start over from scratch.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:56.229
So you could take advantage
of best-in-class offerings, wherever they are

00:14:56.229 --> 00:15:01.099
because you are not deeply locked
in to one provider via provider-specific APIs

00:15:01.099 --> 00:15:04.804
for all the minutiae required
to make things work.

00:15:04.804 --> 00:15:08.321
Your configs, your load balancing,
your service management

00:15:08.321 --> 00:15:09.862
they're the same everywhere.

00:15:10.227 --> 00:15:13.146
So when you have to learn something new

00:15:13.146 --> 00:15:16.549
it's because you chose
a unique service to use

00:15:16.549 --> 00:15:18.885
not because you have to learn again

00:15:18.885 --> 00:15:22.222
how to perform common tasks
in a new cloud

00:15:22.222 --> 00:15:24.644
just to get to the unique service
that you want.

00:15:25.291 --> 00:15:27.694
So Anthos maximizes your ability

00:15:27.694 --> 00:15:30.845
to take advantage
of unique cloud functionality.

00:15:31.775 --> 00:15:35.936
I can't stress enough how big a change
this will be in the industry

00:15:35.936 --> 00:15:39.372
because, until now, adopting cloud
meant choosing a vendor

00:15:39.372 --> 00:15:43.883
being locked in, having only one choice,
and having a heck of a hard time

00:15:43.883 --> 00:15:46.646
integrating with
existing on-premise services.

00:15:46.646 --> 00:15:47.614
No longer.

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:53.620
Anthos revives enterprise computing
with an open, auto-updating software stack

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:55.435
for the next 20 years.

00:15:56.115 --> 00:15:59.025
And unlike previous shifts in technology

00:15:59.025 --> 00:16:02.062
Anthos doesn't require 
an architectural forklift.

00:16:02.062 --> 00:16:03.806
It doesn't force you to rewrite.

00:16:04.363 --> 00:16:08.502
It's one of the rare cases
where it's strategically the right choice

00:16:08.502 --> 00:16:10.412
but also easy to adopt

00:16:10.412 --> 00:16:15.262
with same-year benefits
for your developers and operators.

00:16:16.409 --> 00:16:20.678
We're seeing our Anthos customers thrive,
not just with Anthos itself

00:16:20.678 --> 00:16:22.988
but with a rich and growing ecosystem

00:16:22.988 --> 00:16:28.309
of products, services, partners,
and tools and best practices.

00:16:28.921 --> 00:16:31.224
Some have traditionally focused IT

00:16:31.224 --> 00:16:34.728
others are on the cutting edge
of modern microservices.

00:16:34.728 --> 00:16:39.966
But all are getting better security,
cost savings, better budget control

00:16:39.966 --> 00:16:42.399
and much higher developer velocity.

00:16:43.836 --> 00:16:48.608
So we look forward to more of you
joining this proven new era of innovation.

00:16:48.608 --> 00:16:50.895
And we're eager to help you get started.

00:16:52.311 --> 00:16:54.647
Be sure to catch our live keynote Q&amp;A

00:16:54.647 --> 00:16:57.847
where we'll address top-of-mind questions
from this discussion.

00:16:58.412 --> 00:16:59.393
Thank you.

